<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf8">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="./spec.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/highlight.js/8.4/styles/github.min.css">
<script src="./spec.js"></script>
<pre class="metadata">
title: Transfer Protocol
stage: 0
contributors: James M Snell
</pre>

<emu-intro id="sec-intro">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>This proposal introduces a generalized transfer protocol for JavaScript, allowing objects to move exclusive ownership of their state to new instances. The protocol consists of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A well-known symbol (`Symbol.transfer`) for the transfer protocol method.</li>
    <li>A new essential internal method (`[[Transfer]]`) on all objects.</li>
    <li>Static methods `Object.transfer()` and `Reflect.transfer()` to trigger transfer.</li>
    <li>A Proxy `transfer` handler trap to intercept transfer on Proxy objects.</li>
    <li>Built-in `[Symbol.transfer]()` implementations on iterators, `ArrayBuffer`, `DisposableStack`, and `AsyncDisposableStack`.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The name "transfer" is adopted from the existing `ArrayBuffer.prototype.transfer()` (ES2024).</p>
</emu-intro>

<emu-clause id="sec-well-known-symbols-transfer">
  <h1>Modifications to Well-Known Symbols</h1>
  <p>The following entry is added to the Well-Known Symbols table (<emu-xref href="#sec-well-known-symbols"></emu-xref>):</p>
  <emu-table id="table-new-well-known-symbols" informative caption="Addition to Well-Known Symbols">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Specification Name</th>
          <th>[[Description]]</th>
          <th>Value and Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>%Symbol.transfer%</td>
          <td>`"Symbol.transfer"`</td>
          <td>A method that transfers ownership of an object's state to a new object, rendering the original unusable. Called by the [[Transfer]] internal method.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </emu-table>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-essential-internal-methods-transfer">
  <h1>Modifications to Essential Internal Methods</h1>
  <p>The following entry is added to the Essential Internal Methods table (<emu-xref href="#sec-object-internal-methods-and-internal-slots"></emu-xref>, Table 4):</p>
  <emu-table id="table-new-essential-internal-method" informative caption="Addition to Essential Internal Methods">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Internal Method</th>
          <th>Signature</th>
          <th>Description</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>[[Transfer]]</td>
          <td>( ) **→** Object</td>
          <td>Transfer ownership of this object's state to a new object by invoking the %Symbol.transfer% protocol. Returns the new object. The original object's transferable state is consumed and the original should be rendered unusable by the transfer implementation.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </emu-table>

  <p>The following invariant is added to the Invariants of the Essential Internal Methods (<emu-xref href="#sec-invariants-of-the-essential-internal-methods"></emu-xref>):</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-transfer-invariants">
    <h1>[[Transfer]] ( )</h1>
    <ul>
      <li>The normal return type is Object.</li>
    </ul>
  </emu-clause>

  <p>Because [[Transfer]] is an essential internal method, all objects have it. Standard exotic objects (Array exotic objects, String exotic objects, Integer-Indexed exotic objects, Arguments exotic objects, Bound Function exotic objects, etc.) that do not override [[Transfer]] use the ordinary object definition OrdinaryTransfer (<emu-xref href="#sec-ordinarytransfer"></emu-xref>). Module Namespace exotic objects and other non-extensible exotic objects will throw a *TypeError* from the IsExtensible check within OrdinaryTransfer.</p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-proxy-handler-methods-transfer">
  <h1>Modifications to Proxy Handler Methods</h1>
  <p>The following entry is added to the Proxy Handler Methods table (<emu-xref href="#sec-proxy-object-internal-methods-and-internal-slots"></emu-xref>):</p>
  <emu-table id="table-new-proxy-handler" informative caption="Addition to Proxy Handler Methods">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Internal Method</th>
          <th>Handler Method</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>[[Transfer]]</td>
          <td>`"transfer"`</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </emu-table>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-transfer-abstract-operations">
  <h1>Transfer Protocol Abstract Operations</h1>

  <emu-clause id="sec-ensurenotdetached" type="abstract operation">
    <h1>
      EnsureNotDetached (
        _O_: an Object,
      ): either a normal completion containing ~unused~ or a throw completion
    </h1>
    <dl class="header">
      <dt>description</dt>
      <dd>It throws a *TypeError* if _O_ has been detached by a prior transfer operation.</dd>
    </dl>
    <emu-alg>
      1. If _O_ has a [[Detached]] internal slot and _O_.[[Detached]] is *true*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Return ~unused~.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-ordinarytransfer" type="abstract operation">
    <h1>
      OrdinaryTransfer (
        _O_: an Object,
      ): either a normal completion containing an Object or a throw completion
    </h1>
    <dl class="header">
      <dt>description</dt>
      <dd>It performs the transfer protocol on _O_ by looking up and calling the %Symbol.transfer% method. It rejects non-extensible objects to preserve the immutability contract of `Object.freeze()` and `Object.seal()`.</dd>
    </dl>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _extensible_ be ? IsExtensible(_O_).
      1. If _extensible_ is *false*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Let _transferFn_ be ? GetMethod(_O_, %Symbol.transfer%).
      1. If _transferFn_ is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Let _result_ be ? Call(_transferFn_, _O_).
      1. If _result_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Return _result_.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The `IsExtensible` check rejects frozen and sealed objects. Transfer mutates an object's observable behaviour (from functional to throwing on all operations), which contradicts the immutability contract that `Object.freeze()` / `Object.seal()` establishes. This check is only enforced by [[Transfer]] — a direct `obj[Symbol.transfer]()` call bypasses it.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <emu-note type="editor">
      <p>The `IsExtensible` check creates an inconsistency with `ArrayBuffer`: `buf.transfer()` succeeds on frozen buffers (ArrayBufferCopyAndDetach has no `IsExtensible` check), but `Object.transfer(buf)` would throw. See the proposal README for discussion of the options.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-validatetransfertrapresult" type="abstract operation">
    <h1>
      ValidateTransferTrapResult (
        _target_: an Object,
        _trapResult_: an ECMAScript language value,
      ): either a normal completion containing ~unused~ or a throw completion
    </h1>
    <dl class="header">
      <dt>description</dt>
      <dd>It validates the result of a Proxy `transfer` handler trap, enforcing invariants analogous to those of the `has` and `get` traps for non-configurable properties.</dd>
    </dl>
    <emu-alg>
      1. If _trapResult_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Let _targetDesc_ be ? _target_.[[GetOwnProperty]](%Symbol.transfer%).
      1. If _targetDesc_ is not *undefined*, then
        1. If _targetDesc_.[[Configurable]] is *false* and IsDataDescriptor(_targetDesc_) is *true*, then
          1. If _targetDesc_.[[Writable]] is *false* and _targetDesc_.[[Value]] is *undefined*, then
            1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Return ~unused~.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The invariant enforced above prevents a Proxy trap from fabricating transferability for a target that has explicitly declared itself non-transferable via a non-configurable, non-writable %Symbol.transfer% property with value *undefined*.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-createtransferrediterator" type="abstract operation">
    <h1>
      CreateTransferredIterator (
        _O_: an Object,
      ): either a normal completion containing an Object or a throw completion
    </h1>
    <dl class="header">
      <dt>description</dt>
      <dd>It creates a new iterator object by transferring the iteration-specific internal state from _O_ to a new object with the same [[Prototype]], clears the transferred slots on _O_, and sets _O_.[[Detached]] to *true*.</dd>
    </dl>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _proto_ be ? _O_.[[GetPrototypeOf]]().
      1. If _O_ has [[IteratedArrayLike]], [[ArrayLikeNextIndex]], and [[ArrayLikeIterationKind]] internal slots, then
        1. Let _slotsList_ be « [[IteratedArrayLike]], [[ArrayLikeNextIndex]], [[ArrayLikeIterationKind]], [[Detached]] ».
        1. Let _newIterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(_proto_, _slotsList_).
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[IteratedArrayLike]] to _O_.[[IteratedArrayLike]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[ArrayLikeNextIndex]] to _O_.[[ArrayLikeNextIndex]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[ArrayLikeIterationKind]] to _O_.[[ArrayLikeIterationKind]].
        1. Set _O_.[[IteratedArrayLike]] to *undefined*.
        1. Set _O_.[[ArrayLikeNextIndex]] to 0.
      1. Else if _O_ has [[GeneratorState]], [[GeneratorContext]], and [[GeneratorBrand]] internal slots, then
        1. If _O_.[[GeneratorState]] is ~executing~, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If _O_ has an [[UnderlyingIterators]] internal slot, then
          1. Let _slotsList_ be « [[GeneratorState]], [[GeneratorContext]], [[GeneratorBrand]], [[UnderlyingIterators]], [[Detached]] ».
          1. Let _newIterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(_proto_, _slotsList_).
          1. Set _newIterator_.[[UnderlyingIterators]] to _O_.[[UnderlyingIterators]].
          1. Set _O_.[[UnderlyingIterators]] to *undefined*.
        1. Else,
          1. Let _slotsList_ be « [[GeneratorState]], [[GeneratorContext]], [[GeneratorBrand]], [[Detached]] ».
          1. Let _newIterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(_proto_, _slotsList_).
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[GeneratorState]] to _O_.[[GeneratorState]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[GeneratorContext]] to _O_.[[GeneratorContext]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[GeneratorBrand]] to _O_.[[GeneratorBrand]].
        1. Set _O_.[[GeneratorState]] to ~completed~.
        1. Set _O_.[[GeneratorContext]] to *undefined*.
      1. Else if _O_ has [[AsyncGeneratorState]], [[AsyncGeneratorContext]], [[AsyncGeneratorQueue]], and [[GeneratorBrand]] internal slots, then
        1. If _O_.[[AsyncGeneratorState]] is ~executing~ or ~draining-queue~, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If the number of elements in _O_.[[AsyncGeneratorQueue]] is not 0, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Let _slotsList_ be « [[AsyncGeneratorState]], [[AsyncGeneratorContext]], [[AsyncGeneratorQueue]], [[GeneratorBrand]], [[Detached]] ».
        1. Let _newIterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(_proto_, _slotsList_).
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[AsyncGeneratorState]] to _O_.[[AsyncGeneratorState]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[AsyncGeneratorContext]] to _O_.[[AsyncGeneratorContext]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[AsyncGeneratorQueue]] to a new empty List.
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[GeneratorBrand]] to _O_.[[GeneratorBrand]].
        1. Set _O_.[[AsyncGeneratorState]] to ~completed~.
        1. Set _O_.[[AsyncGeneratorContext]] to *undefined*.
        1. Set _O_.[[AsyncGeneratorQueue]] to a new empty List.
      1. Else if _O_ has [[IteratingRegExp]], [[IteratedString]], [[Global]], [[Unicode]], and [[Done]] internal slots, then
        1. Let _slotsList_ be « [[IteratingRegExp]], [[IteratedString]], [[Global]], [[Unicode]], [[Done]], [[Detached]] ».
        1. Let _newIterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(_proto_, _slotsList_).
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[IteratingRegExp]] to _O_.[[IteratingRegExp]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[IteratedString]] to _O_.[[IteratedString]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[Global]] to _O_.[[Global]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[Unicode]] to _O_.[[Unicode]].
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[Done]] to _O_.[[Done]].
        1. Set _O_.[[IteratingRegExp]] to *undefined*.
        1. Set _O_.[[IteratedString]] to *undefined*.
        1. Set _O_.[[Done]] to *true*.
      1. Else if _O_ has an [[Iterated]] internal slot, then
        1. Let _slotsList_ be « [[Iterated]], [[Detached]] ».
        1. Let _newIterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(_proto_, _slotsList_).
        1. Set _newIterator_.[[Iterated]] to _O_.[[Iterated]].
        1. Set _O_.[[Iterated]] to *undefined*.
      1. Else,
        1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Set _newIterator_.[[Detached]] to *false*.
      1. Set _O_.[[Detached]] to *true*.
      1. Return _newIterator_.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>For generator-based iterators, the [[GeneratorContext]] contains the suspended execution context with all captured state. Transferring this slot moves the entire continuation, including any closed-over variables. The original's [[GeneratorContext]] is set to *undefined* and [[GeneratorState]] to ~completed~ to ensure no shared references to the execution context remain. This is non-trivial engine work.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <emu-note>
      <p>For async generators, the [[AsyncGeneratorQueue]] on the new iterator is always a new empty List, not a copy of the original's queue. The precondition checks ensure the original's queue is empty (both the state check and the explicit queue length check), so no requests are lost.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <emu-note type="editor">
      <p>Map iterators, Set iterators, String iterators, and Iterator Helper results are all created via CreateIteratorFromClosure, and thus have [[GeneratorState]], [[GeneratorContext]], and [[GeneratorBrand]] internal slots. They are covered by the generator-based branch. Iterator Helper results additionally have [[UnderlyingIterators]]. The [[Iterated]] branch covers %WrapForValidIteratorPrototype% objects created by `Iterator.from()` when the argument is not already an instance of %Iterator%.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-ordinary-object-internal-methods-transfer">
  <h1>[[Transfer]] for Ordinary Objects</h1>
  <p>The following internal method is added to the essential internal methods of ordinary objects (<emu-xref href="#sec-ordinary-object-internal-methods-and-internal-slots"></emu-xref>).</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-ordinary-object-transfer">
    <h1>[[Transfer]] ( )</h1>
    <p>The [[Transfer]] internal method of an ordinary object _O_ takes no arguments and returns either a normal completion containing an Object or a throw completion. It performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Return ? OrdinaryTransfer(_O_).
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-proxy-object-internal-methods-transfer">
  <h1>[[Transfer]] for Proxy Objects</h1>
  <p>The following internal method is added to the Proxy object internal methods (<emu-xref href="#sec-proxy-object-internal-methods-and-internal-slots"></emu-xref>).</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-proxy-object-transfer">
    <h1>[[Transfer]] ( )</h1>
    <p>The [[Transfer]] internal method of a Proxy exotic object _O_ takes no arguments and returns either a normal completion containing an Object or a throw completion. It performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Perform ? ValidateNonRevokedProxy(_O_).
      1. Let _target_ be _O_.[[ProxyTarget]].
      1. Let _handler_ be _O_.[[ProxyHandler]].
      1. Assert: _handler_ is an Object.
      1. Let _trap_ be ? GetMethod(_handler_, *"transfer"*).
      1. If _trap_ is *undefined*, then
        1. Return ? _target_.[[Transfer]]().
      1. Let _trapResult_ be ? Call(_trap_, _handler_, « _target_ »).
      1. Perform ? ValidateTransferTrapResult(_target_, _trapResult_).
      1. Return _trapResult_.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>Unlike the `get` and `set` traps, there is no _receiver_ parameter. Transfer has no prototype chain delegation — there is no meaningful "receiver" distinct from the target.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <emu-note>
      <p>[[Transfer]] for Proxy objects enforces the following invariants:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The result of [[Transfer]] must be an Object.</li>
        <li>If the target has a non-configurable, non-writable own property keyed by %Symbol.transfer% whose value is *undefined*, the trap must not produce a result (the target is explicitly non-transferable).</li>
      </ul>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-object.transfer">
  <h1>Object.transfer ( _obj_ )</h1>
  <p>This function is added to the properties of the Object constructor (<emu-xref href="#sec-properties-of-the-object-constructor"></emu-xref>).</p>
  <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
  <emu-alg>
    1. If _obj_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
    1. Return ? _obj_.[[Transfer]]().
  </emu-alg>
  <emu-note>
    <p>Calling `obj[Symbol.transfer]()` directly is valid, but `Object.transfer()` is preferred because it routes through [[Transfer]], which provides argument type checking, protocol validation (`[Symbol.transfer]` must exist and be callable, return value must be an Object), the `IsExtensible` check, and Proxy `transfer` trap dispatch.</p>
  </emu-note>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-symbol.transfer">
  <h1>Symbol.transfer</h1>
  <p>This data property is added to the properties of the Symbol constructor (<emu-xref href="#sec-properties-of-the-symbol-constructor"></emu-xref>).</p>
  <p>The initial value of `Symbol.transfer` is the well-known symbol %Symbol.transfer% (<emu-xref href="#table-new-well-known-symbols"></emu-xref>).</p>
  <p>This property has the attributes { [[Writable]]: *false*, [[Enumerable]]: *false*, [[Configurable]]: *false* }.</p>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-object.prototype-transfer-rejection">
  <h1>Modifications to the Object Prototype Object</h1>
  <p>The Object prototype object (<emu-xref href="#sec-properties-of-the-object-prototype-object"></emu-xref>) is modified to reject %Symbol.transfer% as an own property. This prevents prototype pollution from making every object appear transferable.</p>
  <p>The [[DefineOwnProperty]] internal method of %Object.prototype% is modified so that if the property key is %Symbol.transfer%, the method returns *false* without creating the property. Similarly, the [[Set]] internal method of %Object.prototype% is modified so that when the receiver is %Object.prototype% and the property key is %Symbol.transfer%, the method returns *false* without setting the property.</p>
  <emu-note>
    <p>This follows the model of the thenable curtailment proposal, which proposes similar exotic rejection of *"then"* on %Object.prototype%. Without this restriction, `Object.prototype[Symbol.transfer] = fn` would make every object transferable, analogous to `Object.prototype.then = fn` making every object thenable.</p>
  </emu-note>
  <emu-note type="editor">
    <p>This depends on the approach taken by the thenable curtailment proposal (tc39/proposal-thenable-curtailment). If that proposal stalls or changes approach, this mechanism may need to be revised. Making %Object.prototype% increasingly exotic sets a precedent that future well-known symbols could also follow.</p>
  </emu-note>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-reflect.transfer">
  <h1>Reflect.transfer ( _target_ )</h1>
  <p>This function is added to the Reflect object (<emu-xref href="#sec-reflect-object"></emu-xref>).</p>
  <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
  <emu-alg>
    1. If _target_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
    1. Return ? _target_.[[Transfer]]().
  </emu-alg>
  <emu-note>
    <p>`Reflect.transfer()` is the counterpart for use within Proxy `transfer` traps to forward to the default behaviour, following the pattern of `Reflect.get()`, `Reflect.set()`, `Reflect.deleteProperty()`, etc.</p>
  </emu-note>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-iterator.prototype-transfer">
  <h1>Modifications to the Iterator Prototype Object</h1>
  <p>The following properties are added or modified on the Iterator Prototype Object (<emu-xref href="#sec-%iterator.prototype%-object"></emu-xref>).</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-iterator.prototype-%symbol.transfer%">
    <h1>Iterator.prototype [ %Symbol.transfer% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. If _O_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If _O_ does not have a [[Detached]] internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If _O_.[[Detached]] is *true*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Let _newIterator_ be ? CreateTransferredIterator(_O_).
      1. NOTE: CreateTransferredIterator sets _O_.[[Detached]] to *true* and clears _O_'s iteration-specific internal slots.
      1. Return _newIterator_.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The [[Detached]] slot check ensures that only built-in iterators (which have [[Detached]]) are transferable via this method. Plain objects implementing the iterator protocol with no shared prototype will not gain transfer automatically. Custom iterators that need transfer must implement `[Symbol.transfer]()` explicitly.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <p>The value of the *"name"* property of this function is *"[Symbol.transfer]"*.</p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-iterator.prototype-%symbol.iterator%-modified">
    <h1>Iterator.prototype [ %Symbol.iterator% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>The existing algorithm for `Iterator.prototype[%Symbol.iterator%]()` is modified to add a detach guard. This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. If _O_ is an Object, perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_O_).
      1. Return _O_.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>The detach check ensures that `for...of` throws immediately at the `[Symbol.iterator]()` call rather than silently entering iteration and failing on the first `.next()` call.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-asynciterator.prototype-transfer">
  <h1>Modifications to the %AsyncIteratorPrototype% Object</h1>
  <p>The following properties are added or modified on the %AsyncIteratorPrototype% object (<emu-xref href="#sec-asynciteratorprototype"></emu-xref>).</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-asynciterator.prototype-%symbol.transfer%">
    <h1>%AsyncIteratorPrototype% [ %Symbol.transfer% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. If _O_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If _O_ does not have a [[Detached]] internal slot, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. If _O_.[[Detached]] is *true*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Let _newIterator_ be ? CreateTransferredIterator(_O_).
      1. NOTE: CreateTransferredIterator sets _O_.[[Detached]] to *true* and clears _O_'s iteration-specific internal slots.
      1. Return _newIterator_.
    </emu-alg>
    <p>The value of the *"name"* property of this function is *"[Symbol.transfer]"*.</p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-asynciterator.prototype-%symbol.asynciterator%-modified">
    <h1>%AsyncIteratorPrototype% [ %Symbol.asyncIterator% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>The existing algorithm for `%AsyncIteratorPrototype%[%Symbol.asyncIterator%]()` is modified to add a detach guard. This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. If _O_ is an Object, perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_O_).
      1. Return _O_.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-iterator-disposal-modifications">
  <h1>Iterator Disposal Modifications</h1>
  <p>When the Explicit Resource Management proposal defines `%IteratorPrototype%[%Symbol.dispose%]()` and `%AsyncIteratorPrototype%[%Symbol.asyncDispose%]()`, those algorithms must be modified to handle detached iterators. Disposal of a detached iterator must be a silent no-op — not a thrown exception.</p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-iterator.prototype-%symbol.dispose%-modified">
    <h1>Iterator.prototype [ %Symbol.dispose% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>The algorithm for `%IteratorPrototype%[%Symbol.dispose%]()` is modified to add a detach guard. This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. If _O_ is an Object and _O_ has a [[Detached]] internal slot and _O_.[[Detached]] is *true*, return *undefined*.
      1. Let _return_ be ? GetMethod(_O_, *"return"*).
      1. If _return_ is not *undefined*, then
        1. Perform ? Call(_return_, _O_).
      1. Return *undefined*.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>This ensures that `using` bindings that hold a now-transferred iterator silently no-op at block exit, consistent with `DisposableStack`'s post-`.move()` behaviour and the general post-transfer cleanup contract.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-asynciterator.prototype-%symbol.asyncdispose%-modified">
    <h1>%AsyncIteratorPrototype% [ %Symbol.asyncDispose% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>The algorithm for `%AsyncIteratorPrototype%[%Symbol.asyncDispose%]()` is modified analogously. This function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. If _O_ is an Object and _O_ has a [[Detached]] internal slot and _O_.[[Detached]] is *true*, return *undefined*.
      1. Let _return_ be ? GetMethod(_O_, *"return"*).
      1. If _return_ is not *undefined*, then
        1. Let _result_ be ? Call(_return_, _O_).
        1. Perform ? Await(_result_).
      1. Return *undefined*.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-built-in-iterator-modifications">
  <h1>Built-in Iterator Modifications</h1>
  <p>All built-in iterator types require two modifications to support the transfer protocol:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A [[Detached]] internal slot, initialized to *false*, added to each iterator instance at creation time.</li>
    <li>A detach guard at the start of each `.next()`, `.return()`, and `.throw()` method that throws a *TypeError* if [[Detached]] is *true*.</li>
  </ol>

  <emu-clause id="sec-array-iterator-modifications">
    <h1>Array Iterator Objects</h1>
    <p>The following modifications apply to Array Iterator Objects (<emu-xref href="#sec-array-iterator-objects"></emu-xref>).</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-createarrayiterator-modified">
      <h1>CreateArrayIterator ( _array_, _kind_ )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for CreateArrayIterator is modified to include the [[Detached]] internal slot. It performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _iterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%ArrayIteratorPrototype%, « [[IteratedArrayLike]], [[ArrayLikeNextIndex]], [[ArrayLikeIterationKind]], [[Detached]] »).
        1. Set _iterator_.[[IteratedArrayLike]] to _array_.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[ArrayLikeNextIndex]] to 0.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[ArrayLikeIterationKind]] to _kind_.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[Detached]] to *false*.
        1. Return _iterator_.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-arrayiteratorprototype-next-modified">
      <h1>%ArrayIteratorPrototype%.next ( )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for `%ArrayIteratorPrototype%.next()` is modified to add a detach guard after the internal slot check. The following steps are performed when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
        1. If _O_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If _O_ does not have all of the internal slots of an Array Iterator Instance, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_O_).
        1. Let _array_ be _O_.[[IteratedArrayLike]].
        1. If _array_ is *undefined*, return CreateIteratorResultObject(*undefined*, *true*).
        1. NOTE: The remainder of the existing algorithm is unchanged.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-generator-based-iterator-modifications">
    <h1>Generator-Based Iterator Objects</h1>
    <p>Map iterators, Set iterators, String iterators, and Iterator Helper results are all created via CreateIteratorFromClosure and use the generator machinery. The following modifications apply to these types.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-createiteratorfromclosure-modified">
      <h1>CreateIteratorFromClosure ( _closure_, _generatorBrand_, _generatorPrototype_ [ , _extraSlots_ ] )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for CreateIteratorFromClosure is modified to include the [[Detached]] internal slot in the internal slots list. The relevant steps become:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. NOTE: _closure_ can contain uses of the Yield shorthand to yield an IteratorResult object.
        1. Let _internalSlotsList_ be « [[GeneratorState]], [[GeneratorContext]], [[GeneratorBrand]], [[Detached]] ».
        1. If _extraSlots_ is present, append each element of _extraSlots_ to _internalSlotsList_.
        1. Let _generator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(_generatorPrototype_, _internalSlotsList_).
        1. Set _generator_.[[GeneratorBrand]] to _generatorBrand_.
        1. Set _generator_.[[GeneratorState]] to ~suspended-start~.
        1. Set _generator_.[[Detached]] to *false*.
        1. NOTE: The remainder of the existing algorithm is unchanged.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-generatorvalidate-modified">
      <h1>GeneratorValidate ( _generator_, _generatorBrand_ )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for GeneratorValidate is modified to add a detach guard. This covers all generator-based iterators (Map, Set, String, Iterator Helpers, and user-defined generators). It performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_generator_, [[GeneratorState]]).
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_generator_, [[GeneratorBrand]]).
        1. If _generator_.[[GeneratorBrand]] is not _generatorBrand_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_generator_).
        1. Assert: _generator_ also has a [[GeneratorContext]] internal slot.
        1. Let _state_ be _generator_.[[GeneratorState]].
        1. If _state_ is ~executing~, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Return _state_.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-regexp-string-iterator-modifications">
    <h1>RegExp String Iterator Objects</h1>
    <p>The following modifications apply to RegExp String Iterator Objects (<emu-xref href="#sec-regexp-string-iterator-objects"></emu-xref>).</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-createregexpstringiterator-modified">
      <h1>CreateRegExpStringIterator ( _R_, _S_, _global_, _fullUnicode_ )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for CreateRegExpStringIterator is modified to include the [[Detached]] internal slot. It performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _iterator_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%RegExpStringIteratorPrototype%, « [[IteratingRegExp]], [[IteratedString]], [[Global]], [[Unicode]], [[Done]], [[Detached]] »).
        1. Set _iterator_.[[IteratingRegExp]] to _R_.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[IteratedString]] to _S_.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[Global]] to _global_.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[Unicode]] to _fullUnicode_.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[Done]] to *false*.
        1. Set _iterator_.[[Detached]] to *false*.
        1. Return _iterator_.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-regexpstringiteratorprototype-next-modified">
      <h1>%RegExpStringIteratorPrototype%.next ( )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for `%RegExpStringIteratorPrototype%.next()` is modified to add a detach guard after the internal slot check. The following steps are performed when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
        1. If _O_ is not an Object, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If _O_ does not have all of the internal slots of a RegExp String Iterator Instance, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_O_).
        1. If _O_.[[Done]] is *true*, then
          1. Return CreateIteratorResultObject(*undefined*, *true*).
        1. NOTE: The remainder of the existing algorithm is unchanged.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-wrapforvaliditerator-modifications">
    <h1>WrapForValidIterator Objects</h1>
    <p>The following modifications apply to %WrapForValidIteratorPrototype% objects created by `Iterator.from()` (<emu-xref href="#sec-iterator.from"></emu-xref>) when the argument is not already an instance of %Iterator%.</p>

    <emu-clause id="sec-iterator.from-modified">
      <h1>Iterator.from ( _O_ )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for `Iterator.from()` is modified so that the wrapper object includes the [[Detached]] internal slot. The relevant steps become:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _iteratorRecord_ be ? GetIteratorFlattenable(_O_, ~iterate-string-primitives~).
        1. Let _hasInstance_ be ? OrdinaryHasInstance(%Iterator%, _iteratorRecord_.[[Iterator]]).
        1. If _hasInstance_ is *true*, then
          1. Return _iteratorRecord_.[[Iterator]].
        1. Let _wrapper_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%WrapForValidIteratorPrototype%, « [[Iterated]], [[Detached]] »).
        1. Set _wrapper_.[[Iterated]] to _iteratorRecord_.
        1. Set _wrapper_.[[Detached]] to *false*.
        1. Return _wrapper_.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-wrapforvaliditeratorprototype-next-modified">
      <h1>%WrapForValidIteratorPrototype%.next ( )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for `%WrapForValidIteratorPrototype%.next()` is modified to add a detach guard. It performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[Iterated]]).
        1. Perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_O_).
        1. Let _iteratorRecord_ be _O_.[[Iterated]].
        1. Return ? Call(_iteratorRecord_.[[NextMethod]], _iteratorRecord_.[[Iterator]]).
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-wrapforvaliditeratorprototype-return-modified">
      <h1>%WrapForValidIteratorPrototype%.return ( )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for `%WrapForValidIteratorPrototype%.return()` is modified to add a detach guard. It performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[Iterated]]).
        1. Perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_O_).
        1. Let _iterator_ be _O_.[[Iterated]].[[Iterator]].
        1. Assert: _iterator_ is an Object.
        1. Let _returnMethod_ be ? GetMethod(_iterator_, *"return"*).
        1. If _returnMethod_ is *undefined*, then
          1. Return CreateIteratorResultObject(*undefined*, *true*).
        1. Return ? Call(_returnMethod_, _iterator_).
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-generator-object-modifications">
    <h1>Generator Objects</h1>
    <p>Generator instances are modified to include the [[Detached]] internal slot, initialized to *false*. The detach guard for generators is in GeneratorValidate (see <emu-xref href="#sec-generatorvalidate-modified"></emu-xref>), which is called by GeneratorResume. This covers `.next()`, `.return()`, and `.throw()` on all generator objects.</p>

    <emu-table id="table-generator-instance-slots-modified" informative caption="Modified Internal Slots of Generator Instances">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Internal Slot</th>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>[[GeneratorState]]</td>
            <td>~suspended-start~, ~suspended-yield~, ~executing~, or ~completed~</td>
            <td>The current execution state of the generator.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[GeneratorContext]]</td>
            <td>an execution context</td>
            <td>The execution context that is used when executing the code of this generator.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[GeneratorBrand]]</td>
            <td>a String or ~empty~</td>
            <td>A brand used to distinguish different kinds of generators.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[Detached]]</td>
            <td>a Boolean</td>
            <td>Whether this generator has been detached by a transfer operation. Initially *false*.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </emu-table>

    <emu-clause id="sec-evaluategeneratorbody-modified">
      <h1>RS: EvaluateGeneratorBody</h1>
      <p>The runtime semantics of EvaluateGeneratorBody (<emu-xref href="#sec-runtime-semantics-evaluategeneratorbody"></emu-xref>) are modified to include the [[Detached]] internal slot when creating generator instances:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The internal slots list passed to OrdinaryCreateFromConstructor is changed to « [[GeneratorState]], [[GeneratorContext]], [[GeneratorBrand]], [[Detached]] ».</li>
        <li>After the generator's [[GeneratorState]] is set to ~suspended-start~ and before GeneratorStart is called, the generator's [[Detached]] is set to *false*.</li>
      </ul>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-asyncgenerator-object-modifications">
    <h1>AsyncGenerator Objects</h1>
    <p>AsyncGenerator instances are modified to include the [[Detached]] internal slot, initialized to *false*.</p>

    <emu-table id="table-asyncgenerator-instance-slots-modified" informative caption="Modified Internal Slots of AsyncGenerator Instances">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Internal Slot</th>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>[[AsyncGeneratorState]]</td>
            <td>~suspended-start~, ~suspended-yield~, ~executing~, ~draining-queue~, or ~completed~</td>
            <td>The current execution state of the async generator.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[AsyncGeneratorContext]]</td>
            <td>an execution context</td>
            <td>The execution context that is used when executing the code of this async generator.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[AsyncGeneratorQueue]]</td>
            <td>a List of AsyncGeneratorRequest Records</td>
            <td>Records which represent requests to resume the async generator.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[GeneratorBrand]]</td>
            <td>a String or ~empty~</td>
            <td>A brand used to distinguish different kinds of async generators.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>[[Detached]]</td>
            <td>a Boolean</td>
            <td>Whether this async generator has been detached by a transfer operation. Initially *false*.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </emu-table>

    <emu-clause id="sec-evaluateasyncgeneratorbody-modified">
      <h1>RS: EvaluateAsyncGeneratorBody</h1>
      <p>The runtime semantics of EvaluateAsyncGeneratorBody (<emu-xref href="#sec-runtime-semantics-evaluateasyncgeneratorbody"></emu-xref>) are modified to include the [[Detached]] internal slot when creating async generator instances:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The internal slots list passed to OrdinaryCreateFromConstructor is changed to « [[AsyncGeneratorState]], [[AsyncGeneratorContext]], [[AsyncGeneratorQueue]], [[GeneratorBrand]], [[Detached]] ».</li>
        <li>After the async generator's [[AsyncGeneratorState]] is set to ~suspended-start~ and before AsyncGeneratorStart is called, the async generator's [[Detached]] is set to *false*.</li>
      </ul>
    </emu-clause>

    <emu-clause id="sec-asyncgeneratorvalidate-modified">
      <h1>AsyncGeneratorValidate ( _generator_, _generatorBrand_ )</h1>
      <p>The algorithm for AsyncGeneratorValidate is modified to add a detach guard. It performs the following steps when called:</p>
      <emu-alg>
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_generator_, [[AsyncGeneratorContext]]).
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_generator_, [[AsyncGeneratorState]]).
        1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_generator_, [[AsyncGeneratorQueue]]).
        1. If _generator_.[[GeneratorBrand]] is not _generatorBrand_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Perform ? EnsureNotDetached(_generator_).
        1. Return ~unused~.
      </emu-alg>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-other-builtin-transfer-implementations">
  <h1>Other Built-in Transfer Implementations</h1>

  <emu-clause id="sec-arraybuffer.prototype-%symbol.transfer%">
    <h1>ArrayBuffer.prototype [ %Symbol.transfer% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>This method is added to the properties of the ArrayBuffer prototype object (<emu-xref href="#sec-properties-of-the-arraybuffer-prototype-object"></emu-xref>).</p>
    <p>This method performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Return ? ArrayBufferCopyAndDetach(_O_, *undefined*, ~preserve-resizability~).
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>This delegates to the same abstract operation used by `ArrayBuffer.prototype.transfer()`. Both move the buffer data to a new ArrayBuffer and detach the original.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <p>The value of the *"name"* property of this function is *"[Symbol.transfer]"*.</p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-disposablestack.prototype-%symbol.transfer%">
    <h1>DisposableStack.prototype [ %Symbol.transfer% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>This method is added to the properties of the DisposableStack prototype object.</p>
    <p>This method performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[DisposableState]]).
      1. If _O_.[[DisposableState]] is ~disposed~, throw a *ReferenceError* exception.
      1. Let _moveMethod_ be ? Get(_O_, *"move"*).
      1. Return ? Call(_moveMethod_, _O_).
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>This delegates to `DisposableStack.prototype.move()`, which moves [[DisposeCapability]] (the resource list) from the original to a new stack and sets the original's [[DisposableState]] to ~disposed~. The existing post-`.move()` behaviours — `.use()`, `.adopt()`, `.defer()`, and `.move()` throw *ReferenceError*; `[Symbol.dispose]()` is a no-op — match the transfer contract.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <emu-note type="editor">
      <p>One open question: `.move()` throws *ReferenceError* on disposed stacks, while detached iterators throw *TypeError*. This implementation preserves `.move()`'s existing error type via direct delegation. The alternative is to normalize to *TypeError* for consistency with the rest of the protocol.</p>
    </emu-note>
    <p>The value of the *"name"* property of this function is *"[Symbol.transfer]"*.</p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-asyncdisposablestack.prototype-%symbol.transfer%">
    <h1>AsyncDisposableStack.prototype [ %Symbol.transfer% ] ( )</h1>
    <p>This method is added to the properties of the AsyncDisposableStack prototype object.</p>
    <p>This method performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[AsyncDisposableState]]).
      1. If _O_.[[AsyncDisposableState]] is ~disposed~, throw a *ReferenceError* exception.
      1. Let _moveMethod_ be ? Get(_O_, *"move"*).
      1. Return ? Call(_moveMethod_, _O_).
    </emu-alg>
    <p>The value of the *"name"* property of this function is *"[Symbol.transfer]"*.</p>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>
